                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "\"Plenty: Skyhearth\" is a pointless VR tech demo that looks like it was (badlY) kitbashed out of a bunch of other people's Unity Assets.  All you do in this tech demo is suffer through one of the worst attempts to make an archery game ever made.\n\nWhat do you do in the game?  Very little.  Shoot bow at bird, uninstall quickly.\n\nThe \"game\" here is of a pretty low technical standard. The implementation is basic minimum effort stuff, more like a weekend project rather than a serious, professionally made product... and it's bad even compared to most amateur made games.  An experimental piece... and the experiment is \"Can I spend $100 on Steam Direct and then call myself a game dev?\"\n\nWhile that $100 spend on Steam Direct probably made the developers feel good about themselves, we have to ask why this kind of thing is good for gamers.  It isn't.\n\nI'm not sure if this is an asset flip, but it's one of those cases where the assets just look much better than the rest of the quality of the game... a glaring inconsistency.  There's no evidence, but as always, the biggest problem with asset flips besides the plagiarism is the deplorably low quality of the other aspects of the game, and even if the game assets are genuinely created for the game, the quality of the rest of the game is indistinguishable from that of asset flips.\n\nIt's VR exclusive, and that shows the developer failed to understand the most basic axiom of VR game design... if the only cool thing about your product is VR support, your product is not cool.\n\nThis game is ancient, it's over 10 years old... it's heavily dated and isn't going to give you a remotely contemporary gaming experience... another reason for gamers to be hesitant about adding this to their libraries.\n\nNext is pricing and what value this has for gamers.\n\nA peak of [b]8 concurrent players[/b] (according to SteamDB) for a \"free\" game is a disastrous public reception. Out of 130 million potential players here on Steam, almost nobody was interested.  Even though this is \"free\".  That level of rejection speaks a lot for the quality of the game.\n\nGiven that this free game won't increase your game collector count, is it worth your time to add it to your library?\n\nNot this time.  This isn't worth downloading.  While this won't cost you any money, you get what you pay for, and sometimes, even when the game is free, it's still not enough.\n",
